{"id": "naqsh-relief-moving-light", "verdict": "rewrite", "score": 3, "styleFit": 2, "literal": true, "problems": ["Draws the object itself: a framed carved panel under a teak lintel reads as a picture of a Naqsh wall, not its grammar.", "Leaf and dot motifs turn to mushy clip-art blobs at 320 by 180, and the lattice is busy inside a small band.", "Lattice tiled edge to edge in a box is the pattern-as-wallpaper move the gulf-arabic-first style calls cheap.", "A full 16 second circling sun is a lot of shadow churn for a hero band and competes with any text placed near it."], "fix": "Keep the good idea, depth from cut shadow under raking light, but remove the lintel and the panel frame. Render a sparse, very shallow diamond grid as a soft-edged field that fades out toward the text side, with no leaf glyphs, and let a slow raking light drift a few degrees side to side instead of circling. Stay in two or three tone steps of gypsum with the teak only as a single hairline accent, and name the Bahraini craft in the copy."}
