```json
{"id": "brass-plate-total-glint", "verdict": "rewrite", "score": 3, "styleFit": 3, "literal": false, "problems": ["A whole-element brightness jump to 1.45 reads as a flash, not light catching a brushed edge.", "The saturate boost adds colour pop with no job and pushes toward cheap highlight.", "Scale 1.03 plus brightness is the generic pulse-on-change pattern, with little brass-specific grammar.", "There is no direction to the light, so the tactile lit-object quality of soft-3d-objects is missing."], "fix": "Make the glint a directional sheen. Sweep a narrow masked highlight band across the plate in about 300ms on a cubic-bezier(0.3, 0.7, 0.2, 1), with whole-element brightness capped near 1.1 and saturate removed. Swap the scale pulse for a 1 to 2px translateY lift that settles back with a slight weighted overshoot, so it reads as one heavy, lit, tactile plate."}
```

```json
{"id": "dichroic-slab-flip", "verdict": "rewrite", "score": 3, "styleFit": 3, "literal": false, "problems": ["The slab reads as a flat gradient card with a white border, close to a stock placeholder, not lit glass with depth", "The amber to cyan flip is a linear 35 degree blend, so it looks like a teal and orange gradient rather than an angle-dependent dichroic shift", "There is no refraction, fresnel or thickness, so it misses the believable light that soft-3d-objects needs", "A drawn card inside a card or paywall slot doubles the UI shape and adds no idea of its own"], "fix": "Borrow the dichroic behaviour rather than the slab. Keep the glass mostly neutral and dark. Let a thin-film hue shift (amber at face-on, cyan only at grazing angles) live in a fresnel rim and one narrow internal reflection band that sweeps slowly as the slab tilts, and add real thickness with a refracted, offset edge. Drop the full-face gradient and the drifting stripes, keep one soft cast glow, and aim for dark-luminous with the glass edge as the only light."}
```

```json
{"id": "memphis-squiggle-stripe-edge", "verdict": "rewrite", "score": 3, "styleFit": 3, "literal": false, "problems": ["The thick outlined squiggle is the stock Memphis border motif and reads as a template frame, not a made screen.", "Two framing ornaments, top and bottom, box the content like a sticker border instead of marking one moment of delight.", "The bottom stripe row mixes ink, cobalt and yellow at a tiny scale, so it reads as busy hazard tape, not an even calm beat.", "Edge ornaments on every header, card and slide will repeat into wallpaper, against the brief of delight-only appearances."], "fix": "Keep the calm paper ground and the even-beat repeat, but use one edge and one motif per use: either the squiggle drawn on once with a quick custom ease-out for success and empty states, or an ink-only stripe beat with cobalt as the single accent stripe. Drop the paired top and bottom frame and the tri-colour stripes so it lands as playful-too-fun with tight craft."}
```

```json
{"id": "najdi-triangle-cut-light", "verdict": "rewrite", "score": 3, "styleFit": 4, "literal": false, "problems": ["The cuts are so small and soft at 320 by 180 that they read as tiny arrow glyphs, not openings in a thick wall.", "The light passing behind the wall barely shows. There is no visible glow or spill below the open cuts, so the main idea is lost.", "The thin hairline under the row looks like a generic UI divider and competes with the cuts.", "The first frame is just a flat brown rectangle with a line, with no texture or depth to make the mass feel like plaster."], "fix": "Keep the strict single row and the huge plain mass, but render each cut as a crisp recessed opening with a dark inner edge. Let one slow, warm light band sweep behind so lit cuts glow and cast a short soft spill below, with the teal held back for the leading cut only. Drop the hairline, add a very fine matte plaster grain to the wall, and make the cuts large enough to read at small sizes."}
```

