```json
{"id": "chicha-split-fountain-bars", "verdict": "rewrite", "score": 3, "styleFit": 3, "literal": false, "problems": ["The sheared bars with uneven heights read as an audio equalizer or a hazard stripe, not as chicha block lettering rhythm", "The bars are clean flat vector fills with no ink, grain or misregistration, so the split fountain print process is named but not felt", "The constant sideways slide behaves like a marquee ticker and will pull the eye from header text", "Fluorescent magenta and yellow run the full width of the band at equal weight, so no single part leads"], "fix": "Keep the split fountain roll, but render it as a screen printed band: fine ink grain, a slight magenta and yellow misregistration, and bars cut to the condensed stroke proportions of chicha letters set flush to one baseline instead of jittered heights. Drop the sideways slide or make it a rare stepped shift, and let the gradient roll be the only motion, aiming for retro-pixel-halftone texture on one element."}
```

```json
{"id": "night-puddle-ripples", "verdict": "rewrite", "score": 3, "styleFit": 3, "literal": true, "problems": ["Paints a picture of rain: condensation beads with highlights and a puddle with drop rings, a scene rather than an abstraction of the card.", "The bead band across the top fills the exact area where header text and nav would sit, making it busy behind type.", "Concentric water ripples are a common screensaver look with little of their own grammar.", "The photographic-hero tag does not fit: there is no photograph, just a dark matte field."], "fix": "Remove the glass beads and the puddle reading, and keep only the cadence: one quiet event every few seconds that sends two or three hairline rings in the accent across a flat matte surface, with a custom ease-out decay. Better still, trigger it from the user's tap point so it marks the tap as the card intends. Aim squarely at dark-quiet-matte: tone steps and hairlines, no highlights, with the top band left empty for UI."}
```

```json
{"id": "key-tag-swing-settle", "verdict": "rewrite", "score": 3, "styleFit": 3, "literal": false, "problems": ["Five-step decaying rotate from the top edge is close to the stock animate.css swing, so it reads as a generic attention wobble rather than a crafted settle.", "7deg on a full card or toast is large for a state change in product UI and pulls focus from the new number.", "Four reversals in 420ms feel light and jittery, not like a heavy engraved tag; weight should mean fewer, slower swings with a firmer stop.", "Swinging the whole card can tilt text and controls; it needs to be scoped to the changed element only."], "fix": "Keep the damped pendulum but make it heavy: one swing to about 3deg and one small counter swing near 1deg, then rest, over about 480 to 560ms. Use a slow-out first curve such as cubic-bezier(0.2, 0.8, 0.3, 1) and a firm last step. Apply it only to the tag or number that changed, never the whole card, so it reads as tactile soft-3d weight rather than a wobble."}
```

```json
{"id": "azulejo-truchet-band", "verdict": "rewrite", "score": 3, "styleFit": 3, "literal": false, "problems": ["The grey shade gradient above the band reads as a drop shadow, which breaks the flat-color-field tag and looks muddy on the cream ground.", "The tiles are too small for a 180px frame, and the extra corner squares turn the band into busy noise instead of one rhythm.", "The concrete curve is a faint generic grey stroke, so it does not read as a sweeping modernist form."], "fix": "Drop the shadow gradient and draw the curve as a flat tone step: one lighter concrete plane meeting the ground along the sweep. Enlarge the Truchet quarter-circle tiles about 1.5 to 2 times, remove the corner squares, keep green to one tile in eight, and hold the field to 2 or 3 flat colours."}
```

```json
{"id": "brass-key-tag-sheen", "verdict": "rewrite", "score": 3, "styleFit": 3, "literal": true, "problems": ["The drilled ring hole and engraved rounded inset border draw the hotel key tag itself, so the card reads as a picture of a tag.", "The brushed grain is high contrast and covers the whole frame, so it competes with type instead of acting as a second voice.", "The olive surface lifts toward mid-tone and the brass sheen is broad enough to wash the frame rather than act as one focus.", "There is no real figure carried on the object, so it misses the great version of soft-3d-objects."], "fix": "Remove the ring hole and inset border and keep the tag's grammar: a near-black green ground, a fine anisotropic brushed grain at very low contrast, and one narrow, slow specular sheen, as if heavy metal is catching light. Leave a clear calm zone for the real engraved number set as UI type, aiming at dark-quiet-matte with a single brass accent."}
```

```json
{"id": "sardine-lid-peel-open", "verdict": "rewrite", "score": 3, "styleFit": 2, "literal": false, "problems": ["Reads as a generic top-down clip wipe. Nothing of the key-roll peel survives beyond the direction.", "The idea promises packed rows revealed underneath, but the target is self only, so no row rhythm is delivered.", "The smooth single curve and 6px drift feel soft and default-ish, not the raw, exact character retro-pixel-halftone wants."], "fix": "Borrow the key-roll cadence: advance the clip edge in short ratcheted steps (4 to 6 small pulls on a crisp ease-out, about 380ms total) and stagger the child rows in 30 to 40ms apart as the edge passes them, so the packed rows are the payoff. Drop the soft translateY drift and keep the edge hard and exact, so the stepped, raw-on-purpose character fits retro-pixel-halftone."}
```

```json
{"id": "liner-dusk-horizon", "verdict": "rewrite", "score": 2, "styleFit": 2, "literal": true, "problems": ["Draws a literal half sun, a coastline and a sun reflection on the sea. It is a picture of a sunset, not the poster's grammar.", "Reads as a stock sunset screensaver. The orange disc is the loudest thing on screen and sits where a headline would go.", "The sky is a smooth gradient, not stepped lithograph bands, so it misses flat-color-field. The misregister is barely visible.", "The banded sea is the only real texture idea, and it is muddied by the reflection streaks."], "fix": "Remove the sun, the reflection and the headland. Build the frame from 4 or 5 hard-edged flat bands in 3 colours (navy, teal, cream) with a visible 1 to 2 px print misregister and fine grain, and let one thin orange horizon band be the only accent. Drift the lower strata very slowly and keep the upper half calm for the tall caps headline, aiming at flat-color-field."}
```

```json
{"id": "rib-bind-lashing", "verdict": "rewrite", "score": 3, "styleFit": 3, "literal": true, "problems": ["The twisted rope texture and crossed X binds draw the lashing itself rather than abstracting it, and the X marks read like letter glyphs or sparkles.", "The cream rope is the brightest element and the teal strake lines are almost invisible, so the lens's strict-rib-under-soft-curve grammar barely comes through.", "The sag-to-taut pull is too subtle across the frames to read as a progress state, so the 12s cycle feels like decoration, not a status.", "The illustrative-handmade fit is thin: it is a clean vector line, not a recognisable hand or printed texture."], "fix": "Drop the rope twist and X binds. Keep the 30-unit rib beat and let one curved strake hairline ease from a shallow sag to straight on a custom ease-out, with each rib it crosses settling in sequence to a slightly brighter brass tone and a single #c9a874 point at the leading edge as the only accent. Add a faint printed grain or uneven line weight to the strokes so it earns the illustrative-handmade tag, and keep the band thin and centred with the dark ground calm."}
```

```json
{"id": "launch-trajectory-print", "verdict": "rewrite", "score": 3, "styleFit": 3, "literal": false, "problems": ["The print voice is too faint: the out-of-register offset and the paper grain barely read at this size, so it looks like a flat vector rather than a printed poster.", "The red line cuts through the middle of the cream field, right where a headline would sit, so it fights the text instead of pointing to it.", "The brass quarter circle in the lower left is a third, unrelated shape that clutters a composition meant to have one diagonal.", "The pulse is a small colour shift along the line and gives the motion little purpose."], "fix": "Keep the steep diagonal and the trajectory as the poster grammar, but make the print the second voice: a visible two-plate misregistration on the red line and a coarse halftone or line screen inside the navy wedge. Drop the brass quarter circle and run the line along the wedge edge so the cream field stays clear for one condensed headline, aiming for exact, raw-on-purpose retro-pixel-halftone."}
```

