```json
{"id": "thimble-dimple-dome", "verdict": "rewrite", "score": 2, "styleFit": 2, "literal": true, "problems": ["Draws the thimble itself as a pitted metal ball seen from above. It reads as a golf ball or ball bearing, not a borrowed grammar.", "The object carries no number, label or state, so it is a 3D prop beside the UI rather than a widget.", "The pit grid is faint and the single hot specular dot is a stock sphere shader. The circling light barely registers across frames.", "The red ring is a flat outline floating off the dome, not lit with the metal, so the object stops feeling believable."], "fix": "Keep the fingertip-sized pit grid as a halftone of tiny embossed dimples across the CTA or plan card surface itself, lit by one slow raking light so the pits catch and release. Drop the sphere. Use the red only as a thin focal hairline on the selected state, and aim for soft-3d-objects with the label sitting on the tactile surface."}
```

```json
{"id": "ledger-double-underline", "verdict": "rewrite", "score": 3, "styleFit": 2, "literal": true, "problems": ["Draws the ledger page itself: full-frame ruling, a red margin and column rules read as a picture of accounting paper, not its grammar.", "The double underline is tiny and fixed at one spot near the right edge, so it will not line up with a real total in shipped UI.", "Only the underline is hand-drawn; the rest is mechanical rules, so the illustrative-handmade hand and system barely show.", "Re-inking every 12 seconds turns a closing gesture into a loop that performs for its own sake."], "fix": "Keep the closing-balance gesture: a hand-inked double underline drawn once with a quick, decelerating ink stroke, uniform line weight and slight pressure wobble, then resting. Remove the red margin and full-page ruling, leaving at most a faint band of sparse hairlines on a darker near-black green, and expose the underline position and width as uniforms so it sits under the real total. Aim for illustrative-handmade with one line weight and the cream ink as the only mark."}
```

```json
{"id": "azulejo-tile-set-in", "verdict": "rewrite", "score": 3, "styleFit": 3, "literal": false, "problems": ["Reads as a generic staggered fade, rise and scale-up, the stock list enter, not a tile being pressed into a bed.", "Growing from 0.94 to 1.01 is a pop toward the viewer, the opposite of the downward press-and-seat the idea promises.", "The lens grammar (a pattern that only resolves across four or more cells, a narrow border band) is unused; on a list it is just a 50 ms stagger.", "The 6px rise from below adds a second direction that blurs the single setting gesture."], "fix": "Make the verb a press: start at scale(1.03) with no translate and settle to 1 through a tiny undershoot (0.995) on a firm curve like cubic-bezier(0.3, 0, 0.1, 1), all inside about 260 ms. Let the clip-path open from a thin inset equal to about a quarter of the gap, like grout closing, and stagger in strict row-major grid order, so the full field only reads once a row of four has set."}
```

```json
{"id": "bidri-silver-inlay-band", "verdict": "rewrite", "score": 3, "styleFit": 3, "literal": false, "problems": ["The scroll is a generic double sine braid with beads. It reads as a DNA helix or a rope border, not Bidri's fine wire inlay grammar.", "The silver lines are thick and high contrast against the matte. They look like a stock luxury ornament divider rather than hairline inlay.", "Silver and brass compete as two accents, which works against the single-accent restraint of dark-quiet-matte.", "The polish light reads as a passing glow sweep, which edges toward dark-luminous on a style that should have no glow."], "fix": "Replace the sine braid with true Bidri grammar: a tight repeat of hairline (1 px or less) silver lines set flush and low contrast in the black, such as a stepped vine or a small geometric lattice. Keep a single thin brass rule as the one accent, and limit the polish to a narrow specular glint that lights only the inlaid lines, so it stays matte and quiet."}
```

```json
{"id": "brilliant-facet-plan", "verdict": "rewrite", "score": 3, "styleFit": 4, "literal": true, "problems": ["It draws the round brilliant itself: a top-down facet diagram is a picture of the diamond, used as a hero mark.", "The ring outline and star facets read as a gem icon or a jewellery logo, not as a texture or grammar.", "Restraint is good (near-black, hairlines, one blue glint), but the lit facet patch looks smudgy and low contrast at 320 by 180."], "fix": "Keep the grader's hairline facet geometry but break it out of the circle. Set a sparse triangulated hairline lattice (kite and star angles taken from the cut's real 8-fold proportions) fading across one side of the band, with no ring outline. Let a single crisp fire-blue highlight travel across a few facets on a slow custom curve, and keep the rest near-black so it stays dark-luminous with one focal light."}
